  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nAnother warm one on tap for today as the southerly flow persist for \nmuch of the region. While the warm air advection does not look to be \nas strong today with weaker winds expected we will still see those \ntemperatures climbing into the 70s and 80s across the vast majority \nof the Northland. A cold front will move slowly from northwest to \nsoutheast across our region into tonight. The pacing of the front \nwill be incredibly slow and near stationary at times. As the \nboundary sags south we will see rain chances slowly increase. \nSounding profiles still show a large amount of dry air that will \nneed to be worked over before rain can reaches the surface. The best \nchances (20-40%) for rain at this time will likely stay confined to \nnorthern MN.  \n\nMonday:\n\nThe Northland finds itself within the baroclinic zone as southerly \nwinds across the Midwest meet with the northerly winds out of the \nCanadian Prairies. A few impulses rounding the weak upper ridging \naloft could instigate some light rain across northern MN through the \nfirst half of the day. Better synoptic forcing arrives in the latter \nhalf of the day and into the evening hours as a shortwave aloft\npushes in from the west. Weak instability closer to a surface \nlow moving across Southern MN into Central WI could produce a \nfew rumbles of thunder Monday evening across NW WI. Overall, \nexpecting light rain totals largely between 0.10-0.25\" with rain\nexiting Tuesday morning. \n\nTuesday into the end of the work week:\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region for Tuesday and starts to \ndepart on Wednesday. Quiet weather conditions during this \nperiod with highs in the 50s and 60s and overnight lows in the \n30s. Heading into Thursday and beyond there is a high degree of \nuncertainty among the 00Z suite of guidance. Cluster analysis \nthat favors the EPS still wants to plot a cut off low over the \ndesert southwest that would allow surface high pressure and \nridging to dominate the Great Lakes Region. The clusters more \naligned with the GEFS have a more pronounced upper level trough \nmoving through the Central and Northern Plains that would \nsuggest an active weather pattern for the end of the work week. \nLeveraging the ECAIFS and the AIGFS may be the better option in \nthe extended as they align fairly well and have been fairly \nconsistent with their picture. It is slightly more agreement \nwith the GEFS as the upper level low is less dominate over the \ndesert southwest as a surface low moves across Manitoba and \nOntario bringing a rainy period for the end of the week. For \nnow, will stick with the NBM which continues to highlight PoPs \nof 30-50% through Friday.",
